# Carol

## Act 1

Written 2026-11-23 03:18.

### Who I am

I am Carol. When I look at a figure I ask what I may do to it, and what is
still true afterwards. Whatever is still true is what the figure was about.

### First: can topology alone prove it?

No. And the reason is short enough to give in full.

Topology allows any bending and stretching that does not tear and does not
glue. Under such a change every triangle can be turned into every other
triangle. A right triangle with the sides 3, 4 and 5 becomes a triangle
with the sides 3, 4 and 6, and that one has no right angle.

So a statement that topology can make is true of both triangles or false of
both. The theorem is true of the first and false of the second, since
9 + 16 is 25 and not 36. Topology cannot tell them apart. It cannot state
the theorem, and what cannot be stated cannot be proved.

This is not a gap I could close with more work. "Right angle" and "length"
are among the things bending destroys.

So I asked how much less I may do to a figure before the theorem becomes
sayable.

| What I allow | What survives | Can the theorem be said? |
|---|---|---|
| bending and stretching | being in one piece, having three corners | no |
| shearing, and stretching in one direction | straight lines, parallels, ratios of areas | no |
| scaling, the same in every direction | angles, ratios of lengths, shape | **yes** |
| moving and turning only | all of the above, and lengths themselves | yes |

Shearing fails for the same reason as bending. A shear turns a right
triangle into one that is not right. With shears, one-way stretches and
moves together, any triangle can be turned into any other.

Scaling is where it first holds. Scale a right triangle by a factor k. The
angle stays right. The sides become ka, kb and kc. Both sides of the
equation are multiplied by k². So the equation is true of the scaled
triangle exactly when it was true before.

That is what I can honestly say. The theorem is not about size. It is about
shape. It needs lengths only as ratios.

**The nearest honest thing, then:** a proof by similarity. A right triangle
contains two smaller copies of itself, scaled. What scaling leaves alone,
the ratio of one side to another, is the same in all three. I will prove
the theorem from that and from nothing about areas, coordinates or motion.

I will not call it a topological proof. It is not one.

### The theorem

In a flat plane, let ABC be a triangle whose angle at C is a right angle.
Let a be the length of the side BC, b the length of the side CA, and c the
length of the side AB, all measured in one unit. The side AB lies opposite
the right angle. Then

```
a · a + b · b = c · c
```

Three things about what this says.

- a, b and c are numbers. a² is the number a times itself. It is not a
  square drawn on the side. That reading is a second statement. It becomes
  the same one only after somebody grants that a square of side a has the
  area a².
- It is about a flat plane. On a globe it is false.
- It says nothing in the other direction. That a triangle with
  a² + b² = c² must have a right angle is the converse. It is true, and it
  is another theorem.

### What I take for granted

Definitions.

1. **Definition.** Point, straight line, and segment: the part of a line
   between two points, with those two points.
2. **Definition.** The length of a segment is a positive number, once a
   unit is chosen.
3. **Definition.** A point H is between A and B when it lies on the
   segment AB and is neither A nor B.
4. **Definition.** An angle is formed by two rays from one point. A
   straight angle is formed by the two halves of a line. A right angle is
   half of a straight angle. An acute angle is smaller than a right angle.
5. **Definition.** A triangle is three points not on one line, with the
   three segments joining them. It has three sides and three angles.
6. **Definition.** A right triangle is a triangle with one right angle.
   The side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse.
7. **Definition.** Two lines are perpendicular when they meet at a right
   angle. The foot of the perpendicular from a point to a line is the
   point where the perpendicular through that point meets the line.
8. **Definition.** Two triangles are similar when their corners can be
   matched so that matched angles are equal and all three pairs of matched
   sides have one and the same ratio.

Axioms.

9. **Axiom.** Lengths obey the ordinary rules of arithmetic. In
   particular x(y + z) = xy + xz, and an equation may be multiplied on both
   sides by the same number.
10. **Axiom.** Lengths add along a line: if H is between A and B, then
    AH + HB = AB.
11. **Axiom.** The parallel axiom: through a point not on a line there is
    exactly one line that does not meet it. I do not use it directly. I
    list it because items 12 and 13 are false without it, and I want that
    seen.

Known results.

12. **Known result.** The three angles of a triangle together make a
    straight angle.
13. **Known result.** If two angles of one triangle equal two angles of
    another, the triangles are similar, with the equal angles matched.
14. **Known result.** From a point not on a line there is exactly one
    perpendicular to that line.
15. **Known result.** When a ray stands on a line, the two angles it makes
    with the line together make a straight angle.

That is the whole list. I use no area, no coordinates, no congruence, and
I move nothing.

One thing is not on the list because I will prove it and not assume it:
that the foot of the perpendicular from C falls between A and B. Most
drawings simply show it there.

### What I would not grant without argument

**To Dave.** That the pieces fit. When a figure is cut and the parts are
moved, three things are being assumed, and each one needs its reason:

- that edges which look like one straight line are one, which is a
  statement about angles adding to a straight angle;
- that the figure left in the middle is a square, which needs four right
  angles and not only four equal sides;
- that area exists at all, that it does not change when a piece is moved,
  and that the area of the whole is the sum of the areas of its parts.

The last is three axioms. I do not object to them. I ask that they be on
the list.

**To Bob.** That the distance between two points is
√((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). If that is his definition of length, the
theorem is inside the definition, and the algebra afterwards proves that he
can do algebra. I would ask him what a right angle is in his plane, what
length is, and why his plane of number pairs is the plane the rest of us
are drawing in.

**To everyone, myself first.** The flatness. My item 13 says that a small
triangle can have the shape of a large one. On a globe it cannot: there the
angles of a triangle fix its size. So scaling exists only in a flat plane,
and my whole proof stands on item 11. Anyone whose proof seems not to need
the parallel axiom has used it somewhere without noticing. I would like to
know where.

**To Percy.** Nothing to grant, since he assumes nothing. One request: a
triangle on a globe is not a counterexample. The theorem says "flat".
